{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for Draft3 exact6f8a94ccb3f43db85ea44cb4a781cde944264f9e, sole primary artifact965dd474. Full note, D2 delta and relevant destination/driver/reservation source reviewed. Design only.\n\nF8 P2 — section4.10 overclaims the relay's cancellation power. Its list says recall of an already-sent push is not guaranteed without the relay, then says the relay still does these when it arrives. The existing compromised handler sets aborting, opens credential revocation and a deciding read; it cannot recall an admitted/in-flight Git write. destination.ts seenDecides records published when the host ref is the integration, including after aborting; token revocation is not rollback. Remove recall from the relay guarantees and distinguish preventing a future send, revoking a credential, and discovering an already-effective send. Add the race witness: push sent, compromised notice admitted, host confirms integration; the retained outcome remains published/known or unresolved until read, never falsely cancelled. D1's precise admitted-read/credential-lifetime limit is useful; keep the guarantee stated that narrowly, rather than immediate cessation of all publication after revocation.\n\nF9 P2 — D1 is more than adding observation rows to unchanged mint behavior. On bad standing the proposal opens both revoke and a deciding read. Current mint data permits only revoke (destination.ts475/570); current base and @4 mint rules declare at most1 operation (1882/2372). That new branch needs the revised declared effects/operation closure and destination reservation/holder budget, or the proposed outcome faults/exceeds what was reserved instead of performing its abort/cleanup. Also mint serves first-head, push, receipt and staging/deletion attempts (servedBy/targetOf; destination-host.ts write path). State exactly which served-write cases get key observations and the abort branch, so revoked/unavailable standing does not gate the cleanup or receipt work needed to finish an earlier publication. Replace adds-no-operation/unchanged-cleanup claims with this explicit D1 prerequisite, and require a full-destination-bound witness for the new branch plus successful cleanup/receipt after compromised standing. The separate D1 review must verify these closures before partition Source.\n\nArchitecture — the proposed fresh standing gate is a plausible bounded alternative to an always-available relay. Correct seed-route preconditions and explicit one-shot sendAgain now match namespace.ts and operator.ts. F1-F6 design answers remain intact: reserved seal, stable control state, recovery cases, separately bounded root handoff history, direct indexes and no claimed completion deadline. Directory10 avoids membership9. C1/C2/D1 still need their own adopted contracts/definition revisions; A1 remains separate.\n\nSecurity — a mint-outcome read within10s is a freshness guarantee at that entry, with an explicitly later send and finite credential lifetime, not a send-time revocation fence. Keep that distinction and F8's in-flight limit. Root/partition affiliation and current membership must still be proved by Q6/Q7's first-Source authority witness. No new Source, transport drop or cancellation guarantee is accepted here.\n\nSimplification — retaining the open control state and using a scoped standing read avoid selector rewrites and an unbounded relay reserve. Define the D1 served-write discriminator and closure once, alongside the existing token rules; do not add an independent token/custody mechanism. Section8's destination-definitions-unchanged line should also be narrowed to old pins, because D1 is explicitly a new definition.\n\nStatic source/design comparison and diff whitespace check only. No code edits, tests/gate, provider calls, push or deployment. No existing Source approval is changed by this design verdict.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:965dd474aadc65d35b3fb0512dc5f996906c6ccd","